# screenshot-to-story

The founder sends a photo from his phone with three words. The job is to turn
that into an issue an agent can act on tomorrow without asking him anything.

## 1. Read the image, do not guess at it

Say what is actually visible: the screen, the URL bar, the error text, the app
chrome. Quote text verbatim. If the text is blurred or cut off, say "unreadable"
rather than filling it in. An invented error string sends the next agent to the
wrong file.

## 2. Locate it

```bash
rg -n --no-heading -S '<the exact visible string>' ~/Documents/code/{{ estate.name }} | head -20
```

If the string is in the codebase, name the `file:line` in the issue. If it is
not, say so - it may come from a dependency or a service, and that is useful.

## 3. Ask the one question that matters, in the issue

Not in a reply to him. Put it in the issue body under "Unknown". He answers it
when he gets to it, and the issue is still filed.

## 4. File it

```bash
gh issue create -R {{ github.slug }} \
  --title "<what is broken, in his words where possible>" \
  --label triage \
  --body "$(cat <<'BODY'
## From a screenshot
<what is visible, verbatim>

## Where this likely lives
<file:line, or "not found in the repo">

## Unknown
<the one question>

## Suggested next step
<one line - what an agent would do first>
BODY
)"
```

Reply to him with the issue number and nothing else.

## Never

Do not add `agent-go`. A screenshot is a report, not an instruction. The founder
moves it to `agent-go` when he wants it worked on - that label is the leash.
